<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polonnaruwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polonnaruwa is a city in the North Central Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> that served as the island's capital from the 11th to the 13th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> ruled from it after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayabahu-i">Vijayabahu I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-i">Parakramabahu I</a> kept it as the Sinhalese capital, adorning it with palaces, irrigation tanks and the rock-cut Buddhas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gal-vihara">Gal Vihara</a>. Its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa.jpg" alt="Polonnaruwa" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polonnaruwa_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Polonnaruwa is the main town of Polonnaruwa District. The modern town, also called New Town, lies about 3 km south of the archaeological zone, and the other part of Polonnaruwa is the royal ancient city of the Kingdom of Polonnaruwa. In ancient times it was also called Pulathisipura and Vijayarajapura. The origin of the name is unknown; it was perhaps a contraction of Pulastya nagara, the city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sage Pulastya.</p><p>The town lies in the low-country dry zone, with a tropical climate and seasonal rainfall brought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoons</a>. The district receives an average of about 1,584 mm of rain a year. The Mahaweli River and the region's irrigation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> supply water for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Polonnaruwa was first established as a military post by the Sinhalese kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a> renamed it Jananathamangalam after invading the country, and Raja Raja Chola I built there a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple named after his queen, Vanavan Mahadevisvaram, now known as Siva Devale. The northern and central regions were governed directly as a Chola province under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a>. Chola rule ended after 1070, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayabahu-i">Vijayabahu I</a>, sending three armies against the city, besieged it for seven months and became its ruler.</p><p>Trade and agriculture flourished under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-i">Parakramabahu the Great</a>, who insisted that not a single drop of water falling from the heavens should go to waste. Irrigation systems far superior to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> period were built, the largest being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakrama-samudra">Parakrama Samudra</a>, the Sea of Parakrama. Apart from his immediate successor Nissankamalla I, later monarchs were less strong-willed and prone to quarrels within the court, and their marriage alliances with stronger South Indian kingdoms came to supersede the local royal line. This prompted an invasion by the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga-magha">Kalinga Magha</a> in 1214, which destroyed Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa by fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ancient city of Polonnaruwa has been declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>. Toque macaques have lived in the ruins since the time of human occupation and have continued to thrive long after the humans departed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polonnaruwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chola Empire under Rajaraja I took Anuradhapura in 993, and in 1017 Rajendra I captured King Mahinda V. The Sinhalese capital later moved to <b>Polonnaruwa</b>, beginning the Polonnaruwa Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>A kingdom in the Jaffna peninsula emerged after Kalinga Magha's invasion of <b>Polonnaruwa</b> around 1215.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After Kalinga Magha's invasion devastated the <b>Polonnaruwa</b> kingdom around 1215, Sinhalese resistance gathered in the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dambadeniya">Kingdom of Dambadeniya</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Chola Empire conquered the Anuradhapura Kingdom in the early 11th century, it ruled northern Sri Lanka from <b>Polonnaruwa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/polonnaruwa-kingdom">Polonnaruwa Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the <b>Polonnaruwa</b> kingdom in the 13th century, the dry zone between the Jaffna peninsula and the Sinhalese heartland was left largely to local military chiefs known as Vanniyars.</blockquote><a href="/state/vanni-chieftaincies">Vanni chieftaincies</a></li></ul></section>
